{"id":"y11-7-l5","label":"7 L5: Searching Algorithms","steps":[{"id":"do-now","label":"Do Now: WHILE Loops","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y11l5-do-now","validatorKey":"y11l5_do_now","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Do Now: WHILE Loops</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Use what you learned about WHILE loops in the previous lesson.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-do-now-a\">(a) State what is tested before the first pass of a WHILE loop. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-do-now-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"do-now-a\" aria-label=\"Do Now part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-do-now-b\">(b) State how many times a WHILE loop's body runs if its condition starts FALSE. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-do-now-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"do-now-b\" aria-label=\"Do Now part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y11l5-do-now-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 2 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y11l5-do-now-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"},{"id":"title","label":"Searching Algorithms","content":"<div class=\"lesson-title-slide\"><p class=\"lesson-title-kicker\">Algorithms</p><h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Searching Algorithms</h2><p>Year 11, Unit 7 L5</p></div>"},{"id":"teacher-talk-1","label":"Recap: Linear Search","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Recap: Linear Search</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">You already met linear search: checking every item, one at a time, from the start.</p><ul class=\"lesson-facts\"><li>A linear search checks Numbers[1], then Numbers[2], then Numbers[3], and so on in order.</li><li>A Found flag, set to FALSE before the search, is switched to TRUE the moment a match is seen.</li><li>The search still checks every remaining item after a match, unless the algorithm is written to stop early.</li><li>Today's focus is applying that idea to real, messier algorithms, then meeting a much faster way to search.</li></ul>"},{"id":"activity-1","label":"Debug a Linear Search","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y11l5-activity-1","validatorKey":"y11l5_activity_1","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Debug a Linear Search</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">This algorithm is meant to search a list of customer accounts by AccountID and output the matching customer's name. It has four errors.</p><div class=\"lesson-code\"><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">01&nbsp;&nbsp;DECLARE Accounts : ARRAY[1:1000, 1:2] OF STRING</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">02&nbsp;&nbsp;DECLARE Row : INTEGER</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">03&nbsp;&nbsp;DECLARE AccountID : INTEGER</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">04&nbsp;&nbsp;OUTPUT \"Please enter an account ID \"</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">05&nbsp;&nbsp;OUTPUT AccountID</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">06&nbsp;&nbsp;FOR Row &lt;- 1 TO 1000</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">07&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;CASE OF AccountID = Accounts[Row, 1]</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">08&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;THEN</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">09&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;OUTPUT \"The customer's name is \", Accounts[1, 2]</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">10&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ENDIF</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">11&nbsp;&nbsp;NEXT Row</div></div><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-activity-1-a\">(a) Line 03 declares AccountID as the wrong data type, since account IDs contain letters and numbers. Write the corrected line. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-activity-1-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"activity-1-a\" aria-label=\"Activity 1 part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-activity-1-b\">(b) Line 05 should read the AccountID typed by the user, not output it before it has a value. Write the corrected line. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-activity-1-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"activity-1-b\" aria-label=\"Activity 1 part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-activity-1-c\">(c) Line 07 uses the wrong pseudocode statement to test a single condition. Write the corrected line. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-activity-1-c\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"activity-1-c\" aria-label=\"Activity 1 part c\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-activity-1-d\">(d) Line 09 always outputs the name from row 1, instead of the row that was actually matched. Write the corrected line. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-activity-1-d\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"activity-1-d\" aria-label=\"Activity 1 part d\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y11l5-activity-1-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 4 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y11l5-activity-1-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div><p class=\"exam-source\">Adapted from Cambridge IGCSE 0478, May/June 2026 Paper 22, Question 5</p></div>"},{"id":"activity-2","label":"Name It and Improve It","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y11l5-activity-2","validatorKey":"y11l5_activity_2","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Name It and Improve It</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Even with your four corrections, the algorithm still stops silently if no account matches.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-activity-2-a\">(a) State the name given to this type of search algorithm. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-activity-2-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"activity-2-a\" aria-label=\"Activity 2 part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-activity-2-b\">(b) Describe the two changes you would make so the algorithm outputs a message when the account ID is not found. [2]</label><textarea id=\"y11l5-activity-2-b\" class=\"lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"activity-2-b\" aria-label=\"Activity 2 part b\" spellcheck=\"false\" rows=\"3\"></textarea></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y11l5-activity-2-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 3 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y11l5-activity-2-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"},{"id":"teacher-talk-2","label":"Why Search Faster? Binary Search","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Why Search Faster? Binary Search</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">A linear search over a million sorted values could need a million comparisons. Binary search rules out half the remaining values with every single comparison.</p><ul class=\"lesson-facts\"><li>Binary search only works on data that is already sorted.</li><li>It compares the search value to the middle item of the current range.</li><li>If the middle item is too small, the whole lower half is ruled out in one step.</li><li>If the middle item is too big, the whole upper half is ruled out in one step.</li><li>Low and High track the current range; the search ends when Low passes High or a match is found.</li></ul>"},{"id":"teacher-talk-2-trace","label":"Trace a Binary Search: Value Found","type":"stepper","stepperId":"y11l5-stepper-1","stepperKey":"binary_search_trace_found","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Trace a Binary Search: Value Found</h2><div id=\"y11l5-stepper-1\" class=\"lesson-stepper\"></div>"},{"id":"activity-3","label":"Practice: Trace a Binary Search","type":"embedded-app","appId":"trace-table-practice","embedContainerId":"y11l5-embed-3","embedQuery":"support=1","embedView":"practice","algorithm":{"title":"Binary Search","context":"The sorted array numbers holds [2, 5, 9, 14, 22, 31, 47, 58]. The user searches for 22.","code":["numbers = [2, 5, 9, 14, 22, 31, 47, 58]","target = 22","low = 1","high = 8","found = False","while low <= high and not found:","    mid = (low + high) // 2","    if numbers[mid - 1] == target:","        found = True","    elif numbers[mid - 1] < target:","        low = mid + 1","    else:","        high = mid - 1","print(found, mid)"],"cols":["Line","low","high","mid","found","Output"],"answers":[{"Line":"3","low":"1","high":"","mid":"","found":"","Output":""},{"Line":"4","low":"","high":"8","mid":"","found":"","Output":""},{"Line":"5","low":"","high":"","mid":"","found":"False","Output":""},{"Line":"7","low":"","high":"","mid":"4","found":"","Output":""},{"Line":"11","low":"5","high":"","mid":"","found":"","Output":""},{"Line":"7","low":"","high":"","mid":"6","found":"","Output":""},{"Line":"13","low":"","high":"5","mid":"","found":"","Output":""},{"Line":"7","low":"","high":"","mid":"5","found":"","Output":""},{"Line":"9","low":"","high":"","mid":"","found":"True","Output":""},{"Line":"14","low":"","high":"","mid":"","found":"","Output":"True 5"}]},"content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Practice: Trace a Binary Search</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Complete the trace table. Record a row whenever low, high, mid or found changes, or output occurs.</p><div id=\"y11l5-embed-3\" class=\"lesson-embed\"></div>"},{"id":"teacher-talk-3-trace","label":"Trace a Binary Search: Value Not Found","type":"stepper","stepperId":"y11l5-stepper-2","stepperKey":"binary_search_trace_not_found","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Trace a Binary Search: Value Not Found</h2><div id=\"y11l5-stepper-2\" class=\"lesson-stepper\"></div>"},{"id":"formative","label":"Check Your Understanding","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Check Your Understanding</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Questions to ask around the room:</p><ul class=\"lesson-facts\"><li>What must be true about the data before you can use a binary search?</li><li>How is Mid calculated from Low and High?</li><li>Why does a binary search rule out half the array with every comparison, not just one item?</li><li>What does it mean when Low becomes greater than High?</li><li>For 1000 sorted values, roughly how many comparisons would a binary search need at most, compared to a linear search?</li></ul>"},{"id":"plenary","label":"Plenary: Debug Another Linear Search","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y11l5-plenary","validatorKey":"y11l5_plenary","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Plenary: Debug Another Linear Search</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">This algorithm repeatedly searches Contacts[] by first name until the user chooses to stop. It has five errors.</p><div class=\"lesson-code\"><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">01&nbsp;&nbsp;DECLARE Contacts : ARRAY[1:500, 1:4] OF REAL</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">02&nbsp;&nbsp;DECLARE Row : INTEGER</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">03&nbsp;&nbsp;DECLARE Column : INTEGER</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">04&nbsp;&nbsp;DECLARE Continue : BOOLEAN</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">05&nbsp;&nbsp;DECLARE Stop : BOOLEAN</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">06&nbsp;&nbsp;DECLARE FirstName : STRING</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">07&nbsp;&nbsp;Continue &lt;- TRUE</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">08&nbsp;&nbsp;Stop &lt;- FALSE</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">09&nbsp;&nbsp;WHILE Continue</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">10&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Row &lt;- 1</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">11&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;OUTPUT \"Enter a first name \"</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">12&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;OUTPUT FirstName</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">13&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;REPEAT</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">14&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;IF Contacts[Row, 1] = FirstName</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">15&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;THEN</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">16&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FOR Column &lt;- 1 TO 4</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">17&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;OUTPUT Contacts[Row, 1]</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">18&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;NEXT Column</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">19&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Stop &lt;- TRUE</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">20&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ELSE</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">21&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Row &lt;- Row + 1</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">22&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ENDIF</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">23&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;NEXT Stop OR Row &gt; 500</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">24&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;OUTPUT \"Search for another name? (Y or N)\"</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">25&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;INPUT Answer</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">26&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;IF Answer = 'N' OR Answer = 'n'</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">27&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;THEN</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">28&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Continue &lt;- TRUE</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">29&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ENDIF</div><div class=\"lesson-code-line\">30&nbsp;&nbsp;ENDWHILE</div></div><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-plenary-a\">(a) Contact names are text, so line 01 declares the wrong data type. State the correct data type only. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-plenary-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-a\" aria-label=\"Plenary part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-plenary-b\">(b) Line 12 should read the FirstName typed by the user, not output it before it has a value. Write the corrected line. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-plenary-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-b\" aria-label=\"Plenary part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-plenary-c\">(c) Line 17 always outputs column 1, instead of stepping through all four columns with Column. Write the corrected line. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-plenary-c\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-c\" aria-label=\"Plenary part c\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-plenary-d\">(d) A REPEAT loop must close with a different keyword than the one used on line 23. Write the corrected line. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-plenary-d\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-d\" aria-label=\"Plenary part d\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-plenary-e\">(e) Line 28 sets Continue to the value that keeps the outer loop running forever, even after the user chooses to stop. Write the corrected line. [1]</label><input id=\"y11l5-plenary-e\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-e\" aria-label=\"Plenary part e\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y11l5-plenary-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 5 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y11l5-plenary-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div><p class=\"exam-source\">Adapted from Cambridge IGCSE 0478, October/November 2025 Paper 22, Question 2</p></div>"},{"id":"plenary-2","label":"Plenary: Why Might It Still Fail?","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y11l5-plenary-2","validatorKey":"y11l5_plenary_2","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Plenary: Why Might It Still Fail?</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Even with all five corrections applied, the algorithm can still fail to find a contact that is genuinely stored.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-plenary-2-a\">(a) Explain why the algorithm may not always give the expected results. [2]</label><textarea id=\"y11l5-plenary-2-a\" class=\"lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-2-a\" aria-label=\"Plenary 2 part a\" spellcheck=\"false\" rows=\"3\"></textarea></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y11l5-plenary-2-b\">(b) Explain how you could improve the algorithm so the search is more likely to find the required results. [2]</label><textarea id=\"y11l5-plenary-2-b\" class=\"lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-2-b\" aria-label=\"Plenary 2 part b\" spellcheck=\"false\" rows=\"3\"></textarea></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y11l5-plenary-2-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 4 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y11l5-plenary-2-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div><p class=\"exam-source\">Adapted from Cambridge IGCSE 0478, October/November 2025 Paper 22, Question 2(c)</p></div>"}],"validators":{"y11l5_do_now":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*(the\\s+)?condition\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"WHILE always tests its condition before running the loop body."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*(0|zero|none|no times)\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"If the condition is false on the very first test, the body never runs."}],"y11l5_activity_1":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*DECLARE\\s+AccountID\\s*:\\s*STRING\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Account IDs contain letters and numbers, so AccountID must be a STRING, not an INTEGER."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*INPUT\\s+AccountID\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"The line should read the value the user types, not output a value AccountID has not been given yet."},{"suffix":"c","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*IF\\s+AccountID\\s*=\\s*Accounts\\[\\s*Row\\s*,\\s*1\\s*\\]\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"CASE OF is not used to test a single condition like this - use IF."},{"suffix":"d","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*OUTPUT\\s+[\"'‘’]The customer['‘’]s name is\\s*[\"'‘’],\\s*Accounts\\[\\s*Row\\s*,\\s*2\\s*\\]\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"The output must reference Accounts[Row, 2], the row that was actually matched, not a hardcoded row 1."}],"y11l5_activity_2":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*linear\\s*search\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Checking every item in order, one at a time, from the start, is called a linear search."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*.{0,200}\\bfound\\b.{0,200}\\b(flag|boolean|true|false)\\b.{0,300}\\b(not\\s*found|no\\s*match|not\\s*been\\s*found|wasn'?t\\s*found|was\\s*not\\s*found)\\b.{0,200}$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Think about the Found flag pattern from Year 8: set Found to FALSE before the loop, TRUE on a match, then check IF NOT Found after the loop to output a not-found message."}],"y11l5_plenary":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*STRING\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Names are text, so Contacts should be declared OF STRING, not OF REAL."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*INPUT\\s+FirstName\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"The line should read the value the user types, not output a value FirstName has not been given yet."},{"suffix":"c","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*OUTPUT\\s+Contacts\\[\\s*Row\\s*,\\s*Column\\s*\\]\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Inside the FOR Column loop, each output should use Column, not a hardcoded 1."},{"suffix":"d","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*UNTIL\\s+Stop\\s+OR\\s+Row\\s*>\\s*500\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"A REPEAT loop must close with UNTIL, not NEXT."},{"suffix":"e","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*Continue\\s*(<-|←)\\s*FALSE\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Answering N should stop the outer loop, so Continue must be set to FALSE here, not TRUE."}],"y11l5_plenary_2":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*.{0,300}\\b(not\\s*found|no\\s*match|never\\s*(says?|tells?|outputs?)|nothing\\s*(is\\s*)?output|no\\s*message|silently|not\\s*(been\\s*)?found)\\b.{0,300}$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Even after the corrections, nothing is ever output if the name genuinely is not in the array - the algorithm just moves on to asking for another search."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*.{0,300}\\b(case|upper\\s*case|lower\\s*case|exact\\s*match|trim|space|partial|contains|multiple|more\\s*than\\s*one|another\\s*(contact|person))\\b.{0,300}$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Consider: the search only matches an exact, case-sensitive first name - a small difference in how a name was typed in, or two contacts sharing a first name, would cause problems."}]},"pseudocodeValidators":{}}